  <div id="deposit-at-a-glance" class="deposit-fact-grid">
    <article>
      <strong>Category</strong>
      <p>Done-for-you workflow implementation layered around the systems your team already uses.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Best for</strong>
      <p>Property managers and rental operators whose move-out evidence, approvals, and refund status span several tools.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Verified trigger</strong>
      <p>A confirmed move-out or other client-approved event opens the case and starts the internal review path.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Primary output</strong>
      <p>An approved disposition package with evidence lineage, delivery state, refund milestone, and activity history.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>AI's role</strong>
      <p>Organize records and draft neutral observations linked to the evidence a manager can inspect.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Staff's role</strong>
      <p>Decide policy, liability, wear, charge amounts, refund amounts, legal questions, exceptions, and final approval.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Money movement</strong>
      <p>Off by default. It requires a separately approved payment integration, permissions, controls, and authorization.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Legal scope</strong>
      <p>Operational controls using client-approved rules. The workflow is not legal advice or a compliance guarantee.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="deposit-decision-boundaries" class="wrap detail-section">
  <p class="eyebrow">Authority map</p>
  <h2>What automation, AI, and authorized staff each control</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    A safe deposit workflow separates repeatable coordination from financial and legal judgment. The boundary is explicit
    at every step, not buried in a general disclaimer.
  </p>
  <div class="deposit-table-shell" role="region" aria-label="Security deposit decision boundary table" tabindex="0">
    <table class="deposit-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Workflow decision</th>
          <th scope="col">Automation may</th>
          <th scope="col">AI may</th>
          <th scope="col">Authorized staff must</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Open and identify the case</th>
          <td>Use a verified trigger and attach the correct property, unit, resident, ledger, source dates, and owner.</td>
          <td>Flag possible identity or record mismatches for review.</td>
          <td>Resolve uncertain identity, ownership, or jurisdiction context.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Compare evidence</th>
          <td>Pair verified inspections, images, invoices, messages, and policy files.</td>
          <td>Draft neutral descriptions of visible or documented differences and cite the source records.</td>
          <td>Decide whether the evidence is reliable, relevant, and sufficient for a proposed charge.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Apply rules and dates</th>
          <td>Apply the active, versioned client configuration and calculate internal target dates.</td>
          <td>Summarize a configured rule but not interpret law or invent a deadline.</td>
          <td>Approve the policy, legal source, jurisdiction, effective date, and exception path with counsel as needed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Prepare deductions and refund math</th>
          <td>Reconcile the deposit held, credits, proposed charges, and proposed refund and block mismatches.</td>
          <td>Help organize supporting evidence; suggestions remain nonbinding.</td>
          <td>Determine liability, wear, allowed charges, exact amounts, credits, and the final refund.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Approve and fulfill</th>
          <td>Freeze the reviewed version, generate the approved artifact, and track delivery, refund, and writeback states.</td>
          <td>Summarize the final record without changing the approved decision.</td>
          <td>Approve the frozen proposal and separately authorize any document delivery, money movement, or PMS writeback.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="deposit-required-inputs" class="wrap split-section">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Required inputs</p>
    <h2>What must exist before a case can be manager-ready?</h2>
    <p>
      The evidence standard is configured by portfolio, jurisdiction, property, or policy version. The workflow should
      show what is present, missing, stale, or unverified rather than filling gaps with assumptions.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="check-list">
    <div><span>✓</span><p>Verified property, unit, resident, move-out event, source dates, and assigned reviewer.</p></div>
    <div><span>✓</span><p>Deposit held, credits, unpaid balances, proposed charges, and refund reconciliation from approved records.</p></div>
    <div><span>✓</span><p>Move-in and move-out inspections, paired images when required, and source identifiers for every file.</p></div>
    <div><span>✓</span><p>Invoices, receipts, estimates, work descriptions, lease or policy files, and relevant resident correspondence.</p></div>
    <div><span>✓</span><p>Active policy version, jurisdiction context, deadline source, internal target, and required approval role.</p></div>
    <div><span>✓</span><p>Delivery destination, refund method or tracking state, retention rule, and supported PMS writeback path.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="deposit-exceptions" class="wrap detail-section">
  <p class="eyebrow">Failure behavior</p>
  <h2>What happens when evidence is missing, conflicting, or late?</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    A useful workflow is defined by what it refuses to guess. These are default control patterns; the client's approved
    rules determine the production behavior.
  </p>
  <div class="deposit-exception-grid">
    <article>
      <h3>No move-in inspection or photo</h3>
      <p>The case records an evidence gap. AI must not infer the original condition or convert a move-out image into proof of tenant responsibility.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Ledger and deposit balance disagree</h3>
      <p>Reconciliation fails visibly, the proposal remains unapprovable, and the case routes to the designated accounting owner.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Invoice or receipt is missing</h3>
      <p>The proposed line item shows the missing support and follows the client's approved estimate, exclusion, or manager-review rule.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Evidence changes after approval</h3>
      <p>The prior approval is attached to its frozen version. A material change creates a new version and requires review again.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Deadline risk appears</h3>
      <p>The case escalates by the configured internal target with the unresolved items, responsible owner, source date, and current state visible.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>The PMS cannot provide direct access</h3>
      <p>The design falls back to supported exports, inbox parsing, middleware, a secure review queue, or manual writeback instead of claiming an integration that does not exist.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>The AI result is uncertain</h3>
      <p>The observation is marked for review or omitted. Confidence never substitutes for source evidence or human authority.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>A provider or workflow step fails</h3>
      <p>The failure is logged, the case remains incomplete, and the assigned fallback owner receives the context needed to continue safely.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="deposit-comparison" class="wrap detail-section">
  <p class="eyebrow">Category comparison</p>
  <h2>How this differs from other security deposit products</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    These categories can complement one another. Capabilities vary by vendor, so confirm the actual product, permissions,
    jurisdictions, and operating model before buying.
  </p>
  <div class="deposit-comparison-grid">
    <article>
      <h3>Deposit management platform</h3>
      <p><strong>Typical focus:</strong> collection, escrow or FBO accounts, interest, statements, and digital refunds.</p>
      <p><strong>EMC2Ops focus:</strong> the cross-system evidence, reconciliation, exception, approval, and audit workflow around the disposition.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Inspection or photo-comparison app</h3>
      <p><strong>Typical focus:</strong> condition capture, before-and-after comparisons, signatures, and inspection reports.</p>
      <p><strong>EMC2Ops focus:</strong> connect that evidence to the ledger, invoices, policy version, charge proposal, manager decision, delivery, and refund state.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>PMS-native deposit features</h3>
      <p><strong>Typical focus:</strong> the resident ledger, accounting transaction, attachment, check, or system-of-record entry.</p>
      <p><strong>EMC2Ops focus:</strong> coordinate records that also live in inspection tools, storage, email, vendor systems, approval queues, and fulfillment paths.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Security deposit alternative</h3>
      <p><strong>Typical focus:</strong> replace or reduce the upfront cash deposit through a bond, policy, guarantee, or payment option.</p>
      <p><strong>EMC2Ops focus:</strong> improve the operating workflow for a deposit or move-out claim; it is not itself a financial substitute for a cash deposit.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Generic no-code automation</h3>
      <p><strong>Typical focus:</strong> move fields or trigger actions between apps.</p>
      <p><strong>EMC2Ops focus:</strong> implement a versioned case, evidence requirements, frozen approvals, exception ownership, failure logging, and measurable completion states.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="deposit-comparison-grid__emphasis">
      <h3>When the EMC2Ops approach is useful</h3>
      <p>Use it when the hard part is not only holding or refunding money, but proving how a case moved from scattered records to an authorized, evidence-backed disposition.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="deposit-implementation" class="wrap detail-section">
  <p class="eyebrow">Implementation</p>
  <h2>How a security deposit workflow is scoped, tested, and launched</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    EMC2Ops does not promise a universal launch date before the systems, permissions, evidence rules, and approval boundaries
    are known. A written scope should establish the sequence below.
  </p>
  <ol class="step-list deposit-implementation-steps">
    <li><strong>Trace a real case.</strong> Map one recent move-out from trigger through ledger, evidence, approval, delivery, refund, and writeback.</li>
    <li><strong>Define the control set.</strong> Document required evidence, rule sources, roles, target dates, exceptions, retention, and access limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify integrations.</strong> Confirm supported APIs, exports, webhooks, inboxes, storage, queues, and fallback paths before promising automation.</li>
    <li><strong>Build a reviewable case.</strong> Configure evidence lineage, reconciliation, versioning, approval states, outputs, and failure logs.</li>
    <li><strong>Run acceptance tests.</strong> Test ordinary cases plus missing evidence, conflicting balances, stale approvals, permission failures, and deadline risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Launch with review.</strong> Start with conservative human gates, inspect completed and failed cases, then expand only after the operating record is reliable.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="deposit-implementation-grid">
    <article>
      <h3>What your team supplies</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Representative completed and exception cases.</li>
        <li>Counsel-approved policies and primary rule sources.</li>
        <li>Evidence requirements and deduction authority.</li>
        <li>System owners, credentials, permissions, and test access.</li>
        <li>Approval roles, escalation contacts, and acceptance criteria.</li>
        <li>Retention, delivery, refund, and writeback requirements.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>What changes the price</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Number of workflows, portfolios, jurisdictions, and policy variants.</li>
        <li>PMS, inspection, storage, accounting, payment, and delivery systems.</li>
        <li>Availability and quality of supported integration access.</li>
        <li>Case volume, evidence volume, migration, and exception complexity.</li>
        <li>Security, reporting, monitoring, support, and change-control requirements.</li>
        <li>Whether payment or document delivery integrations are included.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Pricing is quote-based. The written scope should state inclusions, assumptions, exclusions, third-party costs, and expansion pricing before implementation.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap split-section deposit-source-policy">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Rule-source policy</p>
    <h2>How state and local requirements enter the workflow</h2>
    <p>
      The production configuration should use the operator's counsel-approved policy and named primary sources—not an AI
      model's memory or an undated blog summary. Each rule should carry a jurisdiction, source, effective date, version,
      approver, and change history. Existing cases retain the rule version applied to them; a rule change is tested and
      approved before it becomes active for new cases.
    </p>
    <p>
      Examples of primary sources include the
      <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&amp;sectionNum=1950.5." rel="noreferrer">California Legislature's Civil Code § 1950.5</a>
      and
      <a href="https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GOB/7-108" rel="noreferrer">New York's official General Obligations Law § 7-108</a>.
      Those links illustrate source discipline; they are not a complete legal guide.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Security and privacy</p>
    <h2>How sensitive evidence is scoped</h2>
    <p>
      The implementation defines which records are needed, where they are stored, who can review them, how long they are
      retained, what is logged, and which provider handles each step. Prefer scoped, revocable access and separate test
      environments where the connected tools support them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Security requirements and certifications must be verified for the actual client architecture. See the
      <a href="/security/">EMC2Ops security and workflow controls</a> and <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for the
      current public boundaries.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="deposit-buying-checklist" class="wrap detail-section">
  <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation checklist</p>
  <h2>Questions to ask any security deposit automation provider</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    A demo should prove the operating record, not only the happy path. Ask the provider to show each answer using a sample
    case and your actual integration constraints.
  </p>
  <ol class="deposit-buying-list">
    <li>Can every observation and proposed charge link back to the exact inspection, image, invoice, message, or ledger source?</li>
    <li>What happens when the move-in record, forwarding detail, invoice, receipt, or required image is missing?</li>
    <li>Does a ledger mismatch block approval, and who receives the exception?</li>
    <li>Is the proposal frozen at approval, and does a later material change invalidate that approval?</li>
    <li>Who owns liability, normal-wear, policy, legal, deduction, credit, and refund decisions?</li>
    <li>Where do jurisdiction rules and target dates come from, and how are rule changes versioned and approved?</li>
    <li>Which PMS and inspection access paths have been verified for this account—not merely listed as possible?</li>
    <li>Can the workflow operate without bank access or automatic money movement?</li>
    <li>How are document delivery, refund fulfillment, failed payments, returned mail, and PMS writeback tracked?</li>
    <li>What data is stored, for how long, in which systems, and under whose permissions?</li>
    <li>What happens when the AI, integration, credential, provider, or scheduled workflow fails?</li>
    <li>Can the complete case history be exported for management review, a resident dispute, an audit, or counsel?</li>
  </ol>
